 { Alexander Areshchenko – Yuri Vovk, Kiev 2011 Learning objective: This game illustrates how White can dominate the game once he gains control of the e5-square. } 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 c5 4. g3 Nf6 5. Bg2 Nc6 6. Ngf3 g6 7. O-O Bg7 8. c3 O-O 9. Re1 Re8 { This move does not help Black; in fact in some variations the rook is better placed on f8. } ( { A better move is } 9... a5 { getting a head start on the queenside. } 10. e5 ( { or } 9... Re8 9. a4 $5 ) 
Nd7 ( { or } 10... Ng4 $5 { which is included as an exercise } ) 
11. d4 { And now Black can play on the kingside with: } cxd4 ( { or play on the queenside with } 11... b5 12. Nf1 b4 { as in Chernobay – Grachev, Moscow 2012 } { _ } ) 
12. cxd4 f6 $5 { It turns out the rook is more useful on f8! } 13. exf6 Qxf6 { With level chances in Chernobay – Demidov, Moscow 2013. } ) 
10. e5 Nd7 11. d4 f6 12. exf6 Nxf6 { We have reached the position of interest. The big question is: ”Can White’s pieces take possession of the e5-square?” The answer is ”Yes” and for this reason White will simply be better. Black is already in serious difficulties. } ( { Worse is } 12... Qxf6 { due to: } 13. Nb3 $1 c4 ( { Or if } 13... cxd4 $6 { then the strong } 14. Nbxd4 $1 { threatens Black in many ways, such as Nb5-c7. } Nxd4 15. cxd4 $16 { With a typical ”dream position” for this structure. } ) 
14. Nbd2 { Followed by the standard plans Nf1-e3-g4-e5, or Bf4 , or h2-h4-h5. White has a very comfortable position, as Black cannot even attempt to get the typical counterplay by attacking the d4-pawn. } ) 
 ( 12... Bxf6 13. Nf1 $14 { is very similar to the game. } ) 
13. h3 { Preventing a potential ...Ng4, and getting ready to invade the e5-square. } ( { It was possible to capture with } 13. dxc5 { but after } e5 { Black gets some compensation for the pawn, as he has good control of the centre. } ) 
 ( { A slightly stronger alternative was } 13. Nb3 { threatening Nxc5 , forcing the c5-pawn to ”state his intentions”. } c4 ( { or } 13. h3 cxd4 14. Nbxd4 Qb6 15. Re2 $14 { followed by Bf4 controlling the e5-square } ) 
14. Nbd2 $14 { White has a comfortable advantage, as in the note above. The key is how well supported the d4-pawn is. } ) 
 ( { In the event of the immediate } 13. Ne5 $6 { Black solves his problems after: } cxd4 14. Nxc6 bxc6 15. cxd4 e5 $1 16. dxe5 Ng4 ( { this is what White wanted to prevent with } 13. h3 ) 
17. Nf3 Nxe5 { With an equal position. } ) 
b6 ( { If } 13... cxd4 14. cxd4 Qb6 { creating direct pressure against the d4-pawn, then } 15. Nb1 $5 { redirecting the knight to c3 . } Bd7 ( { or } 15... Ne4 16. Nc3 $1 Nxd4 17. Nxe4 dxe4 18. Rxe4 Nxf3 19. Bxf3 $14 { with a superior pawn structure } ) 
16. Nc3 Rac8 17. Rb1 $14 { Followed by Bf4 controlling the e5-square. } ) 
14. Ne5 Bb7 ( { Now } 14... Nxe5 $2 { is punished by: } 15. dxe5 $1 { It is usually better to occupy the e5-square with a piece } ( { the standard reply would be } 14... Bb7 14. Rxe5 ) 
 { but here there is a tactical justification: } Nd7 16. Nc4 $1 { This is the key. } Rb8 ( { but not } 16... dxc4 $2 17. Bxa8 $18 ) 
17. Nd6 Rf8 18. f4 $16 ) 
15. Ndf3 { Reinforcing the control of e5. } Nxe5 ( { The alternative } 15... Ne4 { seems tempting, but } 16. Nxc6 Bxc6 17. Bf4 $14 { followed by Ne5 gives comfortable control of e5 . } ) 
16. Nxe5 Nd7 17. Bf4 cxd4 18. cxd4 Rc8 19. Nxd7 Qxd7 20. Be5 $16 { White has a clear advantage for the following reasons: 1. He has undisputed control of the e5-square. 2. The e6-pawn is a long-term weakness. 3. Black’s light-squared bishop is blocked by his own pawns. 4. Black’s kingside may become vulnerable after the typical h3-h4-h5. } Bh6 { Preserving the dark-squared bishop, hoping for some counterplay, although Black does not have much to aim for anyway. } ( { Let’s see another approach: } 20... Bxe5 21. Rxe5 Qc7 { Black intends to play ...Qc2 . } ( { Attempting a c-file invasion with } 21... Rc7 { is refuted by } 22. Bf1 Rec8 23. Bd3 $16 { covering the c2-square. White’s bishop is extremely powerful on d3 , as it supports the idea h3-h4-h5 attacking the g6-pawn. } ) 
22. Qg4 $1 { Attacking the e6-pawn. } Qf7 ( { But not } 22... Qc2 $6 { due to } 23. Rxe6 Qxb2 24. Rae1 { with a fierce attack. For example: } Rf8 25. R6e2 Rc2 ( { or } 25... Qb4 26. Re7 { and wins } ) 
26. Rxc2 Qxc2 27. Re7 Qxf2 28. Kh1 { With a crushing attack against Black’s monarch. } ) 
23. Qe2 $16 { Followed by Rae1 and h3-h4-h5, which is similar to the game. } ) 
21. h4 ( { Another option was } 21. Bf1 $5 { relocating the bishop to d3 . Then } Rf8 22. Bd3 Qf7 23. Qe2 $16 { followed by h3-h4-h5 after the necessary preparation. } ) 
Qf7 22. Bh3 { This is another good route for White’s bishop, pressuring the e6-pawn. } Bc6 23. Qd3 Bd7 24. Re2 Rc6 25. Rae1 { White’s play is rather simple; he improves his position by exerting pressure on the e6-pawn. } ( { Another option was } 25. Kg2 $5 Rec8 26. Rh1 { followed by Bg4 and h4-h5, which is similar to the game. } ) 
a5 ( { It is not advisable to play } 25... Rec8 { due to } 26. Qa6 { threatening the a7-pawn. The point is that after } Be8 $2 { Black loses on the spot after the forcing line: } 27. Bf4 $1 { (threatening Bxe6 and Bxh6) } Bxf4 28. Bxe6 Rxe6 29. Qxc8 Rxe2 30. Rxe2 Kf8 31. gxf4 ) 
26. Kg2 Rc4 { There is nothing productive to do, hence Black begins to play waiting moves while White continues to make progress. } ( { If } 26... Rec8 27. Bg4 Rc1 { , intending to trade a pair of rooks to lessen Black’s problems, then } 28. Rxc1 Rxc1 29. Rc2 $1 Rxc2 30. Qxc2 { followed by Qc7 invading Black’s camp with a near-decisive advantage. } ) 
 ( { Even worse is } 26... Rc1 $2 { due to: } 27. Rxc1 Bxc1 28. Rc2 Bh6 29. Rc7 $18 ) 
27. Bg4 Rc6 28. Rh1 Rec8 29. h5 { Finally we are there! Areshchenko obviously knows this kind of position, and he could already foresee his break back on move 13 when we arrived at this structure. Black’s position is desperate due to the creation of new weaknesses. The most immediate threat is hxg6 winning the bishop on h6. } Bg7 ( { It is impossible to keep lines closed with } 29... g5 { due to } 30. f4 $1 ( { or } 29... Bg7 29. Re3 $5 { followed by Rf3 with a huge advantage } ) 
gxf4 31. Rf1 Qe7 32. Bxf4 Bxf4 33. Rxf4 { with a massive advantage due to Black’s weaknesses and exposed king. } ) 
30. Bxg7 Kxg7 31. Re5 { Not the strongest, but conceptually correct and certainly good enough. } ( { A more direct, but rather complicated, win could be obtained after: } 31. hxg6 $1 hxg6 ( { or } 31. Re5 Qxg6 32. Qa3 $1 { threatening Qe7 or Re5-h5 with a devastating attack, for example: } Qxg4 33. Qe7 Kg8 34. Qxh7 Kf8 35. Qh8 $1 Qg8 ( 35. Re5 Ke7 $2 36. Rh7 Kd6 37. Qe5 ) 
36. Qf6 Ke8 37. Rh8 $18 ) 
32. Qe3 Qf6 ( 32. Re5 Rh8 $2 { loses a rook after } 33. Qe5 ) 
33. Qh6 Kf7 34. Re3 { Threatening Rf3 with a crushing attack. } ) 
Rc2 32. Qe3 Rf8 { Threatening ...Qxf2. } ( { Taking the pawn by } 32... Rxb2 $2 { loses on the spot after: } 33. hxg6 hxg6 ( { or } 33... Qxg6 34. Rg5 ) 
34. Qh6 Kf6 35. Re3 { Threatening Rf3 , and now } Ke7 36. Rf3 Qg8 37. Qg5 Kd6 38. Rh6 { when White’s attack is irresistible. } ) 
33. Bf3 Rxb2 34. hxg6 Qxf3+ { Going into a technically lost endgame, but there was nothing better to do. } ( { If } 34... hxg6 { White gets a winning attack. For example, } 35. Qh6 Kf6 36. Rh4 Ke7 37. Rf4 Qg8 38. Qg5 Kd6 39. Bxd5 $1 { and wins, since } exd5 $2 { fails to } 40. Qe7 Kc7 41. Rxf8 { . } ) 
35. Qxf3 Rxf3 36. Kxf3 hxg6 37. Re2 Rb4 38. Rc2 Ba4 ( { Not } 38... Rxd4 $2 39. Rc7 { and White wins at once. } ) 
39. Rc7+ Kf6 40. Ke3 { Note how the attack against Black’s king continues; the threat is f2-f4, Rhh7 and Rf7#. } Rb2 41. Rhh7 $18 { Black cannot prevent mate in any sensible way. } g5 ( { Or } 41... Be8 42. f4 Kf5 43. Kf3 { (threatening Rf7) } Kf6 44. Rh8 Bb5 ( 44... Bf7 45. Rf8 $18 ) 
45. Rf8 { . } ) 
42. Rh6+ Kf5 43. f3 g4 44. f4 { Black resigns due to the checkmating threat with Rf7. Final remarks 1. Black’s biggest mistake was 11...f6 creating a weak e5-square he would not be able to guard later. 2. White’s plans as exhibited in this game are extremely typical of this structure; they repeat themselves over and over. 3. White’s break h4-h5 was essential in the victory as it forced the creation of a new weakness – the g6-pawn. 4. In retrospect, we realize that having a pawn on g6 should have been a red flag for Black, indicating his position would be bad after 11...f6. Had the pawn been on g7 it would have been harder for White to break on the kingside (e.g. h4-h5 would not hit anything yet). } 
 1-0

