 { Luka Paichadze – Baadur Jobava, Tbilisi 2012 Learning objective: This game is an example of Black’s counterplay based on the typical exchange sacrifice ...Rxf3. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. c3 Nf6 4. e5 Nd5 5. d4 cxd4 6. cxd4 Nc6 7. Bc4 Nb6 8. Bd3 d5 9. Bg5 Be7 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. O-O Bd7 12. Nc3 O-O 13. a3 f5 { Good timing! Black takes the opportunity to make this move, in order to prevent White’s future expansion on the kingside with f2-f4-f5. } 14. exf6 ( { Better was } 14. Ne2 $5 { hoping to play h2-h4 and Nf4 pressuring the e6-pawn: } Be8 15. Nf4 Bf7 16. h4 $14 { With somewhat better chances since the e6-pawn is vulnerable and Black’s bad bishop is currently locked up. } ) 
Rxf6 { The reader should understand that Jobava played 13...f5 knowing he could secure enough counterplay to avoid being dominated as in the previous example. Unlike the previous game, White’s d4-pawn is vulnerable, and Black’s plans (...Be8-h5 or ...Raf8) will only accentuate this problem. In fact, Jobava must have calculated that White is not well prepared to prevent both of these plans. } ( { PH: The computer suggests retaking with the queen: } 14... Qxf6 15. Re1 Be8 { and with ...Bg6 or ...Bh5 coming, Black is fine. } ) 
15. Ne2 $6 { White probably chose this move in order to meet 15...Be8 with 16.Ng3 preventing the annoying ...Bh5, but this move allows a strong continuation. } ( { Instead if } 15. Re1 Raf8 { threatening ...Rxf3 } ( { now } 15. Ne2 Be8 $6 { does not work well due to } 16. Ne5 $1 { ⩲ since } Nxd4 $6 { is met by } 17. Bxh7 $1 Kxh7 18. Qxd4 $14 ) 
16. Re3 { and now: } Be8 $1 { Bringing the bishop into the game via h5 (or g6) (. It is Black who is fighting for the advantage since the pressure down the f-file has become far more important than the backward pawn on e6.) } ) 
 ( { PH: Surprisingly, } 15. Ne5 $1 { ⩲ is the best move, giving up the great e5-square. } Nxe5 16. dxe5 Rf4 17. Ne2 { White keeps a space advantage and the better minor pieces. } ) 
Rxf3 $1 { A natural and strong decision. At the cost of an exchange, Black destroys White’s pawn structure. The main targets are the f-pawns and the d4-pawn. } ( { Another option was } 15... e5 $5 16. Nxe5 Nxe5 17. dxe5 Qxe5 { and the resulting structure will be studied in the next example. } ) 
 ( 15... Be8 { would be well met by } 16. Ng3 { preventing the annoying } Bh5 { . } ) 
16. gxf3 e5 $1 { Planning ...Bh3 to turn the ‘bad bishop’ into a dangerous weapon. Black’s position is not necessarily superior yet, but White’s defence is very hard to conduct due to the multiple threats. } ( { After the imprecise } 16... Qg5 $6 17. Kh1 Qh5 18. Ng3 Qh4 19. Ne2 { Black only gets a draw by repetition after } Qh5 ( { but } 19... e5 $5 { is also a worthy alternative } ) 
 ( { while } 19... Nxd4 $4 { loses to } 20. Nxd4 Qxd4 21. Bxh7 ) 
 ) 
17. Re1 $2 { This is not the best defence, though White’s position was quite difficult to play. } ( { If } 17. dxe5 Nxe5 { the knight becomes active: } 18. Ng3 Rf8 19. Be2 Qh4 $17 { With the unpleasant threats of ...Rf6-h6 and ...Ng6-f4 followed by ...Qh3 . } ) 
 ( { Probably the best defence was the weird-looking } 17. Bb5 { with the intention to trade off the c6-knight before it becomes a threat. } Rf8 18. Bxc6 bxc6 19. Ng3 { Chances are approximately balanced, but Black is really the one playing for an advantage. A possible continuation is } exd4 20. Qxd4 c5 21. Qd1 Qg5 { with excellent compensation, threatening ... h7-h5-h4. } ) 
Rf8 ( { Even stronger was the more direct: } 17... Qg5 $1 18. Kh1 ( { or } 17... Rf8 17. Ng3 $6 Nxd4 { with a winning position due to the threats of ...h7-h5-h4 and ...Rf8 } ) 
Qf6 $1 { Threatening ...Qxf3 with check, to which there is no satisfactory answer. For example: } 19. Ng3 ( { or } 18... Rf8 18. f4 $6 e4 $1 20. Bc2 Bg4 { followed by ...Bf3 with a decisive attack } ) 
Nxd4 20. Re3 Rf8 $19 ) 
18. Ng3 Qf6 { Threatening ...Nxd4. } ( { Instead } 18... Nxd4 $2 { fails tactically due to } 19. Bxh7 $1 Kxh7 20. Qxd4 $16 { thanks to the pin down the e-file. } ) 
19. dxe5 Nxe5 20. Rxe5 $6 { A desperate decision made under pressure. } ( { It was slightly better to keep the exchange with } 20. Be2 { but after } Bc6 $1 { the threat of ...d5-d4 is strong. For example: } 21. Qb3 { To pin the d5-pawn. } Kh8 ( 21. Rxe5 Nbc4 $5 { is a worthy alternative } ) 
 { And now there is nothing better than: } 22. f4 $1 ( { but not } 22. Qe3 $2 { due to } d4 $1 { -+ } ) 
Qxf4 23. Qe3 { Bringing the queen into the defence, and now } d4 24. Qxf4 Rxf4 $15 { with a superior endgame due to the excellent piece coordination and the passed d-pawn. } ) 
Qxe5 { Despite material equality, White’s position is strategically lost since he has weaknesses all over the board, such as the pawns on b2, f3, f2, as well as his vulnerable king. } 21. Qd2 { Giving up a pawn to gain some time to reorganize his forces, but this will not really help. } ( { If } 21. Rb1 { , protecting the b2-pawn, then } Qf4 22. Be2 Bc6 { threatening ...d5-d4 , or ...Nc4-e5 . } ) 
Rxf3 { Black has an extra pawn, a superior structure and a safer king; the rest of the game is rather simple. } 22. Bf1 Rf8 23. Bg2 Re8 24. Rd1 Ba4 25. f4 Qf6 26. Rf1 Bc6 27. f5 Qe5 { Threatening ...Qe3, trading queens into a winning endgame. } 28. f6 gxf6 { White cannot create serious threats. } 29. Qd1 ( 29. Qh6 { doesn’t help due to } Qg5 { . } ) 
Nc4 30. Nf5 Ne3 31. Qh5 Kh8 { Final remarks 1. Black transferred into a French Type I structure under ideal conditions, as he had time to obtain counterplay along the f-file. White would have been better off playing 14. Ne2 instead. 2. Black’s exchange sacrifice was fully justified. White’s kingside was so badly damaged that it was impossible for him to become organized in order to make use of his extra rook. } 
 0-1

