 { Gregory Shahade – Varuzhan Akobian, Philadelphia 2012 Learning objective: This game is an example of Black’s ...e6-e5 break and how to conduct the resulting position. } 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. c3 c5 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. Ne2 Qb6 8. Nf3 cxd4 9. cxd4 f6 10. exf6 Nxf6 11. O-O Bd6 12. b3 { Everything is according to theory so far. } ( { Another common alternative is } 12. Nc3 O-O 13. Re1 Bd7 14. Be3 { when the b2-pawn is untouchable: } Qxb2 $2 15. Na4 $1 Qa3 16. Bc1 Qb4 17. Bd2 Qa3 18. Re3 $1 { +- Followed by Bb1 capturing Black’s queen. } ) 
e5 $2 { This is certainly premature, though the idea is generally present and would deserve more consideration if Black’s development were complete. } ( { More common is } 12... O-O 13. Bb2 Bd7 14. Ng3 Kh8 15. Re1 Rae8 { with roughly level chances. PH: } 13. Bf4 { is more dangerous here, but } Bxf4 14. Nxf4 Ne4 { is a typical idea for Black that gives him enough counterplay. } ) 
13. dxe5 Nxe5 14. Nxe5 Bxe5 15. Rb1 $2 ( { Missing the chance to punish Black’s 13th move. Correct was: } 15. Be3 $1 Qd6 ( 15. Rb1 Qa5 16. Qe1 $1 Qxe1 17. Raxe1 O-O 18. Bd4 $14 ) 
16. Rc1 { Gaining a crucial tempo, and threatening Bc5 with a big advantage. Now the critical variation is: } Bxh2 17. Kh1 Be5 ( 17. Rb1 Ng4 18. Nd4 $1 { (threatening Rxc8) } Nxe3 19. fxe3 Be5 20. Qh5 $18 ) 
18. Bc5 Qc7 19. Nd4 { White’s attack is decisive, for example: } Kf7 20. f4 $1 Bxf4 21. Nb5 Qb8 22. Qf3 Bxc1 23. Qh5 g6 24. Rxf6 $1 Kxf6 25. Qh4 Bg5 26. Bd4 Kf7 27. Qxg5 $18 ) 
O-O { Despite previous mistakes, we reach an interesting and characteristic position for the French Type I structure. Black has an isolani, but unlike those examples we studied in Chapter 1, Black’s d5-pawn is actually a passed pawn. Also unlike Chapter 1, trading pieces or placing a knight on d4 will not help White so much, as we will see in this game. } 16. h3 ( { The natural } 16. Be3 { is refuted by: } Bxh2 $1 17. Kxh2 Ng4 18. Kg3 ( { Even worse is } 18. Kh1 Qd6 $1 { when White’s position is hopeless. For example: } 19. g3 Nxe3 20. fxe3 Qh6 21. Kg1 Qxe3 22. Kg2 Rxf1 23. Kxf1 Bh3 24. Ke1 Rf8 { with forced mate. } ) 
 ( { Or } 18. Kg1 Nxe3 19. fxe3 Qxe3 20. Kh2 Rxf1 21. Qxf1 Qxd3 $17 ) 
