Bd7 17. Bf4 ( { An option was } 17. Be3 Qb4 ( { not } 17. Bf4 Qd6 $2 18. f4 $18 ) 
18. Qe1 Qe7 19. Rd1 a6 20. Qd2 Rae8 { when White has a tiny plus. It is not easy to take control of d4 , as after } 21. Bd4 Bxd4 ( { or } 21. Bf4 Bc7 $5 ) 
22. Nxd4 Nh5 23. Rfe1 Qf6 { Black gains counterplay with ...Nf4 . } ) 
Rae8 18. Qd2 ( { Taking control of } . Qd2 d4 { with } 18. Bxe5 Rxe5 { 19.Nd4? does not work at all, since White’s centre is built on sand, and after 19...Nh5! } ( { but not 19...Qxd4 ?? } 20. Bxh7 $1 { +- } ) 
 { his position quickly collapses: 20.Qd2 } ( { or 20.Nf3 } 20. Qd2 Re7 $17 { followed by ...Nf4 , ...Qf6 with serious threats } ) 
 { 20...Nf4 } 21. Bc2 ( { or } 21. Rfe1 Rg5 $19 ) 
 { 21...Qxd4! -+ } ) 
Kh8 19. Bxe5 Rxe5 20. Ng3 Qd4 21. Qb2 Qxb2 22. Rxb2 Rc8 { Some pieces are off the board, but White has nothing special since the d5-pawn is both a potential weakness and a potential threat. } 23. Rd1 ( { Slightly preferable was } 23. Re2 Rxe2 24. Nxe2 { covering } d4 { , and after } Kg8 25. f3 Kf7 26. Kf2 Ke6 27. Ke3 Kd6 28. Kd4 g6 29. Nc3 a6 { White has a tiny plus, though Black can gain counterplay with ...Be6 and ...Ng8-e7-c6 , lifting the blockade of his passed pawn. } ) 
Kg8 24. Rbd2 Kf7 25. Be2 Rce8 26. Bf3 Re1+ 27. Kh2 Rxd1 28. Rxd1 Be6 { Now a pair of rooks is off the board. If this was the typical isolani we studied in Chapter 1, White would have a big advantage in the spirit of the game Wojtaszek – Fressinet. However, in this position Black’s isolated and passed pawn is actually becoming more of a threat, and White must play precisely to stay in the game. } 29. Nf1 ( { The alternative } 29. Ne2 Ke7 30. Nd4 Bd7 { is similar to the game, and of course trading rooks with } 31. Re1 $6 Kd6 32. Rxe8 $2 Nxe8 { can only favour Black. For example, } 33. Ne2 Nf6 34. Kg1 Kc5 35. a3 Bb5 $5 36. Kf1 Bxe2 37. Kxe2 Kd4 { when the passed d5-pawn and superior king should give Black the win. } ) 
