Nc3 42. Nd3 Kd6 43. Nb2 Ba6 { winning the a2-pawn and later the game. } ) 
41. Ne1 Ba6 $6 { Conceptually correct, but imprecise again. } ( { Better was } 41... Be6 $1 { keeping the advantage, since the line } 42. Bg2 Nd5 43. Bxd5 Bxd5 44. f4 gxf4 45. Nd3 Kd6 46. Nxf4 Be4 { favours Black greatly, because after } 47. Nd3 Bxd3 48. Kxd3 Ke5 { the pawn endgame is winning for Black. } ) 
42. Bg2 h6 ( { If } 42... Nd5 43. Bxd5 Kxd5 44. Nf3 h6 45. h4 $1 { White is just in time, and after } Bc8 46. hxg5 hxg5 47. Nxg5 Bxg4 48. f4 Bf5 $15 { Black’s winning chances are minute. } ) 
43. Kd1 $2 { A fatal waiting move, allowing Black to consolidate his advantage. } ( { White could have saved the game with } 43. f4 $1 { getting rid of the weakness, and after: } gxf4 44. Nd3 Kd6 ( { or } 44. Kd1 Bxd3 45. Kxd3 { and it is Black who must be precise now, for example } Nd7 $6 46. Ke4 $1 { ⩲ } ) 
45. Nxf4 { The endgame is drawn. } ) 
Nd5 $1 { -+ The threats 44...Nc3 and 44...Nf4 force White to trade his light-squared bishop. } 44. Bxd5 Kxd5 { White’s pawns on light squares are easy targets for Black’s bishop. The battle is decided and the rest is simple. } 45. Nf3 ( { If } 45. f3 { then } Ke5 46. Kd2 Bb5 $1 { is the most precise, leaving White in zugzwang } ( { but not } 46. Nf3 Bf1 47. Nc2 Bxh3 $2 48. Ke2 { when the bishop is trapped } ) 
 { . } 47. Nc2 Bf1 48. Ne1 Kf4 49. Nc2 Kxf3 50. Nxd4 Kf4 51. Nf5 Bxh3 52. Nxh6 Bxg4 $19 ) 
Ke4 46. Ne1 ( { Black is also winning after } 46. Nd2 Kd3 47. Nf1 Bb7 48. Ng3 ( { or } 48. Nd2 Bg2 ) 
