Bf1 47. Kd2 d3 48. f3+ ( { Or } 48. Kd1 Bxh3 49. Kd2 Bxg4 50. Nxd3 h5 { and the h-pawn decides the game. } ) 
Kf4 49. Nxd3+ ( 49. Kc1 { doesn’t help: } Ke3 50. Kd1 Be2 51. Kc1 d2 $19 ) 
Bxd3 50. Kxd3 Kxf3 { The pawn endgame is winning for Black. } 51. Kc4 Kg3 52. Kb5 Kxh3 53. Kxa5 Kxg4 54. Kxb4 h5 ( { White resigns. After the line } 54... h5 54. a4 h4 56. a5 h3 57. a6 h2 58. a7 h1=Q $11 { Black’s queen covers the promotion square. Final remarks 1. Throughout the entire game, White was unable to install a firm blockade against Black’s d5-pawn. In fact, this isolated pawn was never vulnerable. 2. Trading pieces did not favour White; in fact towards the end, the trade of rooks allowed Black to obtain a winning endgame as the d-pawn advanced decisively. } ) 

